      "documentSlug": "gene-therapy-in-pd-aav-gdnf-aadc-gad-and-gba1-vectors",
      "documentConcept": "Gene therapy in PD: AAV-GDNF/AADC/GAD and GBA1 vectors",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:b85bbbadbb5a8b18275ae2a9022381ab7f77ec2cb8ce6901951067cfb1408054",
      "text": "Gene therapy for Parkinson's disease uses adeno-associated viral vectors that carry genes such as GDNF, AADC, GAD and neurturin, and, in registered programmes, GBA1. The early-phase GDNF study reports safety findings and exploratory motor changes, and its authors state that a randomized controlled study is planned to confirm them. In a sham-controlled GAD trial the scores fell in both arms, with a greater fall in the gene therapy arm, while a sham-controlled neurturin trial was not superior to sham surgery. A randomized, sham-controlled GDNF study is registered, and a registry record for an intravenous GBA1 programme is withdrawn. A regulator announcement about AADC deficiency concerns a different indication.",
